## Group 3 Peer Review

We really enjoyed reading your paper. Overall, we thought it addressed important questions
about biases and how human and robotic biases can conflict. The topic was especially compelling
because of the difference between what people say they want in a partner and what actually
influences their decisions. The introduction provided a strong background with relevant studies,
and the research questions were clear and connected well to the overall motivation.
One of the strongest aspects of the report was the modeling approach. Creating four
different models to answer the research questions proposed at the beginning was a clear strength,
and comparing weighted and unweighted logistic regression models was well suited to those
questions. We also particularly appreciated that the training was repeated over 100 random splits.
This made the results more reliable and helped ensure the model was as accurate as possible given
the information provided, without leaning heavily toward one specific group. Including the
baseline accuracy was also helpful for understanding how well the models performed. We found
the discussion especially strong, particularly the explanation of your findings, given that your
conclusions differed from your original hypothesis. The warnings on how the data should be
interpreted are also highly applicable to this class and to the dangers of treating data as objective
when it does not tell the full story. We also valued the connection drawn between racial bias and
the ways in which dating algorithms could reinforce existing stereotypes, which relates well to
topics we have discussed in class.
As for criticism, we have relatively little, and most of it concerns providing more detail on
the data and results rather than changing the overall approach. The main area in which we wanted

more detail was the dataset itself. We may have overlooked this, but we were uncertain as to what
data was used. The paper states that it comes from a Columbia University speed dating experiment,
so we wondered whether the Columbia article was the source of the data. Stating more explicitly
where the data originates would help validate the findings, ideally with a direct citation, the date of
data collection, and the number of participants involved. The paper explains how the data cleanup
took place, but we remain curious about what data was actually used. Since rows with missing
values were removed, we were also curious how many observations were excluded and whether
this could have affected the results. Additionally, we questioned whether the same participants
appeared in multiple speed dating interactions and, if so, whether this was accounted for when
splitting the data into training and testing sets.
Regarding the research questions, we noted that RQ3 mentions race, gender, and age, but
the analysis focuses only on race. We saw that this was acknowledged in the limitations, but we
wondered whether you plan to include gender and age in the final project or to adjust the research
question. Including sample sizes for each racial group would also provide more context for the
comparisons, particularly if some groups had fewer observations. The figures were helpful, though
slightly more descriptive captions would make them easier to interpret. We also believe that
presenting the regression coefficients for RQ2 and RQ4 in tables would make the results easier to
compare. One inconsistency we observed is that the Methods section specifies a significance level
of 0.05, whereas the Discussion states that statistical significance was not tested. Clarifying this in
the Methods or including the significance results, if available, would make the report more
consistent. We were also interested in the unexpected negative coefficients for sincerity and
ambition, and we believe that discussing possible explanations would strengthen that section.

Overall, we found the paper to be well organized and easy to follow. We especially
appreciated the acknowledgment of the dataset’s limitations and the care taken when interpreting
the racial differences. We look forward to seeing how the project develops in the final paper.
